pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% Regression test (wrong verdict): a `def_order` whose `fresh_right` list is
% EMPTY used to define a deliberately non-transitive relation, and one `dom`
% then chased its 3-cycle down to the empty contradiction. The formula is
% exactly-one-of-three and `x1 = 1, x2 = 0, x3 = 0` satisfies it -- see the
% companion exactly_one_of_three_satisfiable under correct/version3 -- yet this
% used to verify as `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE`.
%
% Nothing validated the transitivity variable lists.
% `OrderContext::get_transitivity_substitution` zips `left`, `right` and
% `fresh_right` with `izip!`, which TRUNCATES to the shortest list, so an empty
% `fresh_right` collapses BOTH substitutions to the identity. `order_proof` then
% pushes every definition constraint as a premise and as its own proofgoal, so
% `rup >= 1` discharges "transitivity" for an arbitrary relation.
%
% The relation below is reflexive (`1 ~ui 1 vi 1 vj >= 1` holds at u = v) but
% orders x1 < x2 < x3 < x1. `OrderContext::check_transitivity_variables` now
% rejects the list before the substitutions are built. The same order with a
% correctly sized AND fresh list is still rejected, but for the right reason --
% the transitivity proofgoal genuinely fails RUP.
f 4;
def_order cyc
  vars
    left u1 u2 u3;
    right v1 v2 v3;
    aux;
  end;
  def
    1 ~u1 1 v1 1 v2 >= 1;
    1 ~u2 1 v2 1 v3 >= 1;
    1 ~u3 1 v3 1 v1 >= 1;
  end;
  transitivity
    vars
      fresh_right;
    end;
    proof
      proofgoal #1
        rup >= 1;
      qed;
      proofgoal #2
        rup >= 1;
      qed;
      proofgoal #3
        rup >= 1;
      qed;
    qed;
  end;
end;
load_order cyc x1 x2 x3;
dom >= 1 : x1 -> x2 x2 -> x3 x3 -> x1 : subproof
  proofgoal #4
    rup 1 ~x1 >= 1;
    rup 1 ~x2 >= 1;
    rup 1 ~x3 >= 1;
    rup >= 1;
  qed;
qed;
output NONE;
conclusion UNSAT;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
